#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "000408ba-6fbd-51da-9002-82fdde167a1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Point Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuities Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f03d32e3-2489-5993-a57e-aa208bd12465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366714} {:answer/id #uuid "9282a665-b4cb-5be5-9d4d-e69c05c2f4fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366715} {:answer/id #uuid "242e2566-f841-5914-8fd2-d09e23fe77b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366716} {:answer/id #uuid "c46dc117-1222-557b-a17c-5e2d1ec23648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366717} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0351aa-de1a-5cf0-bc4c-c6833fb4eec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "242e2566-f841-5914-8fd2-d09e23fe77b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366716} :answer-field/id #uuid "4129dafb-6e62-56bc-b31b-dac8d699d22a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e66f2c1c-a94b-5afe-ae18-b6390689133c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104419" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/assets/ae097e5f002d970ffe7393cacce708d431f4ef1f46c5a2c95e3fa1f5846d2c7d.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=4.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(4)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)\\ne f(4)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=4$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(1)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-1$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally, the function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=3$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ and $x=4$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a76f8129-1676-55b0-a8fc-af97551fb634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366721} {:answer/id #uuid "6599e070-e6ac-5600-8194-8e03a9ed440e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366722} {:answer/id #uuid "9454118f-c8d7-52a9-abfc-aaffae21de7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366723} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad59a51-e359-5bc5-8bf2-0991e32220b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366724} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca873b1-9369-5163-bfa7-9a8ee1f9110f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ad59a51-e359-5bc5-8bf2-0991e32220b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366724} :answer-field/id #uuid "41002051-f769-5b30-b6d1-d0945b88e5c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f08cee0d-f749-5e56-a39d-99fa204b19b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-48120" :question/problem "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-1 & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ 2(x-1), & x>1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3x+2, & x<1 \\\\ 4, & x=1 \\\\ -3x+8, & x>1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-1}, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=1:$\n\n- $f(1)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={1}^{3}-1=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =2(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=1:$\n\n- $g(1)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & =3(1)+2=5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & =-3(1)+8=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=5.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)\\ne g(1)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=1$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=1:$\n\n- $h(1)={e}^{(1-1)}=1$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}h(x) & ={e}^{(1-1)}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}h(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}h(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}h(x)=h(1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo, the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, only $g(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0023f1b9-6c00-567a-a7cf-e5921a5a56af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366728} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e08aeb-7145-54b9-aad5-bbf0696d2ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366729} {:answer/id #uuid "c58e21ab-9d20-59c7-bcdf-0232f6160590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366730} {:answer/id #uuid "eed26057-9bcd-5424-989a-fa8eab8884ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366731} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc4d0b4-4b54-5744-bc72-35f147f1460b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0023f1b9-6c00-567a-a7cf-e5921a5a56af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366728} :answer-field/id #uuid "a234af1b-c94e-5649-b7b8-320b90e2ae3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7fcd771-d957-5c34-bd60-9ce3836ceaea" :question/math-academy-id "q-48123" :question/problem "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+3 & x<-1 \\\\ 4, & x=-1 \\\\ 3x+4, & x>-1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1), & x<-1 \\\\ 0, & x=-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-1, & x>-1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x+1, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x) & =2(-1)^{3}+3=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x) & =3(-1)+4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $g(-1)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1+1)=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x) & =(-1)^{2}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=g(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo,  the function $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $h(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}h(x) & =(-1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}h(x) & =2(-1)^{2}+(-1)+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}h(x)=2.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}h(x)=h(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo,  the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, only $f(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "000408ba-6fbd-51da-9002-82fdde167a1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Point Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuities Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efb82a82-e085-54a8-92ae-9b116b2cac4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366735} {:answer/id #uuid "a523529d-d907-5ab6-9dd8-174f84ac5194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366736} {:answer/id #uuid "53de7be4-03ff-57bc-bef8-0ece3faedb12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366737} {:answer/id #uuid "a196ea96-6430-53f6-aed6-142e915ddd7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366738} {:answer/id #uuid "53b13a91-5354-51e3-b12e-39d6ed35bbc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efb82a82-e085-54a8-92ae-9b116b2cac4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366735} :answer-field/id #uuid "24dcb092-25f3-593f-9410-a51f6bc4bec7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ad5208d-6613-538b-805c-388d7abb0069" :question/math-academy-id "q-71067" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/assets/d036e94f6a07c526c9609ca2030cc785927562b0771335bbe12aa5fe27c3e369.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-3)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)\\ne f(-3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nA similar argument applies for $x=3,$ which is also a removable discontinuity. \n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=3$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f869f8d5-2d75-57ad-b701-cef991d0f24c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366742} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad9a713-8c6f-5319-938c-da71e8be6643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366743} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5ddd70-dd75-5119-bd50-a2cb1fb29ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366744} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3b46a7-b87d-58e8-8e36-5632e2207d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366745} {:answer/id #uuid "183e309e-2fa3-581b-af9b-431ffa1777c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ad9a713-8c6f-5319-938c-da71e8be6643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366743} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6fa1780-41fc-5ac9-a457-609624ed0793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40053aa5-090e-5d15-b319-92a57f6d2972" :question/math-academy-id "q-71180" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}4x, & x<2 \\\\ 4, & x=2 \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x>2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f(2)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}(4x)=8, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}{x}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=2$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}]}
